# Waymo/AV perception session inventory: workflow findings

Observation window: through 2026-10-04 18:56:47.276 UTC, based on `observation-context.json`; source base `a553645ef22761aa7708abf1ee109952a6cd2c38`. This is a workflow and trace-coverage review, not a scientific interpretation of model results.

## Coverage and accounting

The two designated roots have 29 pre-cutoff threads in their recursive persisted spawn trees: 23 under `avperc-impl` and 6 under `avperc-frontier`. A separate same-checkout seed query finds 51 threads with cwd `/data02/home/philip.yang/workspace/sureal`: those 29 and 22 `Guardian review` threads. The Guardian threads are auto-review records, not researcher/implementer/reviewer workers. Twenty-one contain indexed allow decisions; one has no indexed decision payload. No denial appears in the retained decision payloads. See `session-inventory.json` for each Guardian thread, decision item/turn IDs, time, and rationale. A text search over title/name/first-user-message/preview for `waymo`, `avperc`, or `perception` finds only the two roots; the cwd expansion is what reveals the Guardian satellites. All other pre-cutoff top-level threads were checked by this metadata query; no additional text-matching root was found. Three frontier children begin after cutoff and are excluded.

All 29 thread rows, indexed item counts, turn statuses, public user/assistant message references, command execution metadata, and projection watermarks are present in the machine-readable inventory. Root user/assistant content was sampled in detail to construct the chronology; child public messages are not transcripted wholesale. Guardian decisions were enumerated as structured public payloads. The analysis did not replay every raw JSONL rollout, so “complete” here means complete discovery within the indexed spawn graph and cutoff-filtered indexed history, not proven complete local transcript recovery. Projection-state ordinals are current, not cutoff snapshots. Legacy/mixed history, projection omissions, and duplicate semantics remain possible. No reasoning items or credentials are included; command strings are omitted while event IDs, timestamps, status, durations, and exit codes remain.

At the roots, the independently calculated cutoff snapshot in `root-accounting.json` reports impl 960 turns, 60 compaction items, 6,759 commands and frontier 41 turns, 8 compactions, 502 commands. A fresh read-only query using `created_at_ms <= 1791140207276` agrees with `root-accounting.json`; command events created after the cutoff are excluded. Root status counts are impl 955 completed, 3 failed, 1 interrupted, 1 in progress; frontier 40 completed, 1 in progress. The impl turn ending in a model-capacity/remote-compaction error has stored duration 27,804,061 ms; that duration includes prior productive work and must not be interpreted as idle time. Likewise, a tool command's recorded duration is not productive effort. Child threads overlap; summing their durations, tokens, or goal time would double-count or misstate work.

## Chronology and workflow

| Phase | Evidence and outcome | Workflow reading |
|---|---|---|
| Sep 29, 18:38–19:38 UTC: define the project, acquire a real slice, establish a tracer bullet | The request ended mid-sentence and Codex asked which deliverable to pursue (`avperc-impl`, turn `01a0ee76-63fc-79e2-9857-19b5d913d8fa`, final item `call_A1wrlwFBozjp6tnyE8hYoxqP`, 18:38:59). User selected an implementation plan, specified Torch/JAX without TensorFlow (`01a0ee7a-672c-7672-bfb7-be4252b9241f`, 18:43), HDFS as full-store and two local scenes (`01a0ee7b-2cd9-7c62-86a5-e105fa272ee5`, 18:44; answer `01a0ee7c-6602-7b60-a9de-590bd58e3525`). The requested end-to-end tracer bullet was initially blocked because checked HDFS locations had no Waymo data and GCS auth was absent (turn `01a0ee83-0610-7073-b6d6-76cfe5262d83`, final `msg_031c4f878d2513b9016abc0978301887d288ac5f971f9e722f`, 18:54). The user ran the hermetic authentication/setup flow (`01a0ee86-226b-7ed3-8eb3-da383371dd9e`, 18:56; completion `01a0ee93-ac19-7c81-8ebe-c438d6f904e3`, 19:10). The resulting report records two scenes, 1.2 GB, all 17 component families, 34 HDFS-verified files, 397 frames and 143,625 records (`01a0ee93-ac19-7c81-8ebe-c438d6f904e3`, final `msg_031c4f878d2513b9016abc13964e7887d28bfaba74e83bc02d`, 19:37). Repo corroboration: Waymo tracer tooling/research landed as commits `05b9192` and `b9a5a9b` on the retrospective base's ancestry. | Strong recovery from a concrete environmental blocker: user performed the credentialed step locally, and the agent used a hermetic script plus checksummed transfer. The initial request was visibly incomplete, so asking which deliverable to pursue was a necessary clarification; the trace does not establish avoidable delay. A reusable source/auth preflight could have reduced uncertainty earlier; any time saved is not measured by these traces. |
| Sep 29 evening–Sep 30: turn data ingestion into a research program with acceptance gates | User corrected the conceptual scope toward Waymo Perception as anchor with Motion and End-to-End Driving as distinct task suites (`01a0eeac-9584-70a3-bdee-9ae222e55f6d`, 19:38); then requested LiDAR, camera, range-view and reference-object lines (`01a0ef18-542f-7a51-9ebb-790c607d7f62`, 21:35; `01a0ef1b-8e64-7353-a9d4-7f7945819c67`, 21:39; `01a0ef1d-92ef-75b1-ac6b-62b08d740814`, 21:41), a multimodal research program (`01a0ef4b-aaa9-7592-b8bd-f1b932571658`, 22:31), and an explicit geometry foundation (`01a0ef50-f285-7cd0-a0e1-845554b7c632`, 22:37). On Sep 30, user required a live Insula verifier at every milestone and made Insula itself the first milestone (`01a0efe4-8912-7ef0-b15f-021ae29d02e3`, 01:18–01:20). They also asked for every research task to have a goal, verifier, and acceptance criteria (`01a0f0e1-863c-7e52-a148-d8186ab09f01`, 05:55). | The workflow turned open-ended research into explicit, testable tasks and brought runtime validity into the definition of done. The user supplied several important domain boundaries and acceptance constraints interactively. These became effective control points, but were introduced over many turns rather than consolidated in an early decision record. |
| Oct 1–2: model debugging and experiment expansion | User queried model size, single-batch fit, and proposed batch-overfit as a first-tier architecture verifier (`01a0f950-19d8-7b80-a379-094948be20f4`, 21:12; `01a0f950-b7f9-7733-b30e-f92fe7b38f5e`, 21:13; `01a0f963-7b75-7f43-9f3d-045e51dc031f`, 21:34). The next day they asked for architectural dimensions, then approved a wider set of architecture ideas and requested full-batch overfit comparisons (`01a0fa4a-a34a-76b1-b7f6-7ed9ae4b1784`, Oct 2 01:46; `01a0fd73-f9a0-7ae3-a393-70a53433f703`, 16:30). They added durable experiment tracking/journaling and “land things as they are ready” (`01a0febf-477c-7213-96f3-69b136196500`, 22:39–22:44). Repo chronology corroborates successive evidence/tooling commits and the eventual architecture study, including `05b9192` for the Waymo tracer/research evidence before the later mainline integration. | Productive pattern: investigate the observed failure, define a bounded verifier, then compare controlled variants and retain results. Improvement: freeze a compact experiment matrix and a named owner/next checkpoint before adding new model variants or infrastructure asks. The request surface broadened from an end-to-end data path to visualization, model architecture, dynamic HDFS, journaling, and task landing in a short window. |
| Oct 3: independent analysis, handoff friction, and landing work | `avperc-frontier` began with “understand the initial experiments” (`01a10076-d095-7e51-942e-d9f9b4aa6851`, 06:32). The user approved transferring its findings into the implementation goal (`01a100a0-0243-7ee2-b6b0-cdfac48085c3`, 07:17). Later the user asked for implementation status, queue contents, and how to send messages to the worker (`01a102a2-708c-7c21-873c-23bcff2f9b56`, 16:39; `01a102aa-5f76-74a1-9050-bb9d3e035bf3`, 16:48; `01a102af-dc3d-7ff1-9134-8e750713f346`, 16:54). At 19:24 they supplied a concrete ordered handoff after the resource gate, with queued follow-up tickets (`01a10327-ed7e-7c82-bab4-27f98b9a48ab`, 19:24). They then noted the PR security-scan failure and asked it be fixed (`01a1033a-2d8e-7043-a74c-4e59f4432674`, 19:25; `01a1034a-49d1-75d2-b15d-02d5100a857b`, 19:42), asked whether it landed, and requested a separate worker for landing (`01a10356-ee1a-7e33-95fd-7d52e5b99016`, 19:56; `01a10357-8458-7942-b074-2ce8d18222f5`, 19:57). Repo history includes verified research closeout `b9a5a9b`. | Parallel analysis and implementation were useful, but cross-thread information transfer was manual and status/ownership were hard to see. The user repeatedly had to ask where work stood and how to contact the worker. Separate landing work was an operational queue problem, not a scientific one. |
| Oct 3 night–Oct 4 morning: collaboration protocol becomes a major workstream | The implementation user asked for first-class models/training structure and later for a file-backed task queue, active worktree map, and landing rule (`01a103f2-8c90-7352-a1c7-29e253c45931`, Oct 3 22:46; `01a10412-b2e7-7d13-8e01-fbfddff27e82`, Oct 3 23:21; `01a10432-eaa8-7850-9f1a-3710841cc558`, Oct 3 23:56; `01a10435-c211-7a53-9887-309f780634a7`, Oct 3 23:59). They then explicitly asked for a repo-local collaboration mechanism, multiple workers, clear task ownership, transcript summaries, worker state/health, and native thread/turn semantics (`01a10457-0c0b-7321-bd16-1aefabeb3d48`, 00:36; `01a10466-458c-7871-9f95-b0bcc27f1b01`, 00:52; `01a1047b-c490-7bd2-9de3-396a5cf74cac`, 01:16; `01a10480-e709-7233-8beb-c2b9a67dfeaf`, 01:21; `01a10485-6a27-7f42-9d0a-69b398431c5c`, 01:26). The user requested the full spec (`01a1048c-7cca-71d0-882e-f148e9e1a7a5`, 01:34) and later permitted review (`01a104a6-a3b8-73a1-8e59-c65e4d912ccf`, 02:03); those events are not treated as implementation admission. Claude’s review of the plan then returned changes required; the user said “fix” (`01a104d5-bf96-7fd3-becf-1820f11fd4f8`, 02:54; `01a104e1-7bbc-7cb2-82f8-d7490e1eecb5`, 03:07). The review led to multiple focused revisions before passing checks and landing. Execution admission was separately recorded in commit `95323a2`; spec/review approval did not itself authorize implementation. The documentation review sequence includes `f9fbd5b`, `d5ead21`, `deed4aa`, `ef0bee5`, and `3e9f366`; the separate execution-admission record is `95323a2`, followed by later mainline integration at `a553645`. | This is an observable review/rework loop: external review found real plan gaps; the plan was corrected and rechecked, and the user requested approval/status updates before landing. Review caught missing sequencing and proof obligations before implementation. But collaboration design and documentation became an extensive workstream while the perception implementation still had unfinished tickets; this audit does not estimate effort from elapsed trace time. |
| Oct 4, 06:34–18:56: remote compaction failure, recovery, and request to resume science with MAC | The impl root turn `01a1059f-6b12-7360-a6cb-6268290a4d1e` started at 06:34:56 UTC and failed at 14:18:20 UTC during remote compaction with “selected model at capacity”; the stored duration is 27,804,061 ms. A status check at 16:32 identified the worker as interrupted and ticket 49 awaiting closure (`01a107c2-9c45-7353-9d8d-96eae83490e9`, answer `msg_01ee33eb2c251b2b016ac2801c523087d2b18cf02d5d78f045`, 16:34). The user asked how to continue (`01a10800-d26a-77e2-be1a-3c967ea8eb96`, 17:40); a recovery handoff started a fresh turn and told it to inspect retained evidence rather than repeat completed effects (answer `msg_01ee33eb2c251b2b016ac28fe2436087d299d53d00b46b34e7`, 17:41). By 18:52 status reported ticket 49 complete/landed, while MAC implementation remained unfinished (`01a10842-7a8e-7b20-b534-deab1363dd18`, answer `msg_01ee33eb2c251b2b016ac2a0ae840087d2ac8e77f1d379da91`, 18:53). At 18:55 user explicitly requested this retrospective to get back to perception research using MAC (`01a10845-a842-7323-98fd-4ed6ac7c889c`, 18:55). Mainline at cutoff is `a553645`; ticket49 implementation commit `cdc2206`, interface correction `c7cea5f`, and Kata binding/task map `d2ebbd3` are in its ancestry. | The error was service capacity during remote compaction, not scientific acceptance or code failure. Recovery depended on a human status inquiry and a new manual handoff. The long stored duration is not evidence of a 7.7-hour hang or downtime. This is precisely where a MAC overview should expose terminal cause, last meaningful progress, retained evidence, next safe action, and whether an automated retry is active. |

## What worked

The strongest execution pattern was evidence first: the data slice was checksum-verified through HDFS, the project used explicit milestone acceptance criteria, and model development later adopted batch-overfit as a fast first-tier verifier. Separate implementation and analysis threads allowed one path to investigate initial results while the other continued the program. The frontier findings were deliberately handed back to the implementation goal and approved by the user. Independent review of the collaboration plan found sequencing and audit gaps that were then addressed before mainline integration. Guardian review payloads provide a separate approval trail, and the repo history shows selected changes progressing through explicit review, verification, merge, and published mainline commits.

## What to improve

The key bottleneck was coordination visibility and transfer, not a lack of simultaneous work. The user repeatedly asked for status, current task, active worktrees, how to message the worker, and what remained before implementation could resume. A useful MAC should make each item below visible from evidence already produced:

- Bind each task/spec to one owning attempt and exact Codex thread, worktree, branch, and base commit; record the handoff recipient and acknowledgement.
- Show a concise trace summary with last verified progress, pending action, blockers, turn terminal state/error, and links to exact evidence items. Separate operating state from health (e.g. waiting on compaction vs stuck vs failed).
- Represent acceptance and landing as explicit gates with verifier results and commit/remote identity; prevent “done” from being inferred from a queued item or a thread's last assistant message.
- Preserve independent analysis and implementation, but publish a structured handoff into the task queue so recommendations do not require manually reconstructing a second transcript.
- Keep one bounded scientific priority queue. Mark infrastructure work, experiment work, and closeout separately, with explicit dependencies and a visible reason when infrastructure temporarily takes priority.
- Detect recoverable model-capacity/compaction failures and expose retry state, without interpreting stored turn duration as inactivity or replaying already landed actions.

The behavioral evidence supports MAC as a way to reduce status polling, manual message relays, ambiguous ownership, and lost handoffs. It does not show that MAC itself improves model accuracy or scientific signal; experiment selection and valid evaluation remain the drivers of those outcomes.
